# Tests of the assembler that do not pass, and why.  run-suite.sh reads
# this: a test named here is expected to fail, and the suite fails if it
# passes, so the line goes in the commit that mends the cause.
#
# name <white space> reason.  AS-T-nnn is the task in docs/as-tasks.md
# that mends it.  A reason beginning SKIP means the test is not run; none
# is so marked now -- the three that built inside the source tree were
# rewritten to build nothing.
#
# Each line was checked by running the test with GNU as in this
# assembler's place: a test GNU passes fails here for a fault of this
# assembler's.  Where GNU failed too, its failure was read.  Three tests
# asked for what GNU does not do and were corrected (an %ss: prefix GNU
# omits, in the two i8086 tests; Intel's lodsd and stosd in AT&T source,
# in test_intel_dual_syntax); the rest fail with GNU only where they ask
# for this assembler's own messages and options, past the fault listed.
#
# Tests whose expectation was stale -- the assembler having since changed
# on purpose -- were corrected rather than listed: test_directive_surface
# (.if and .macro exist), test_x86_64_short_local_temp_branch and
# test_x86_local_temp_branch_runtime (a branch within a section is
# resolved, not relocated), test_lexer_core (string tokens are raw),
# test_output_binary (code is padded with NOPs), test_testing_fuzz_perf
# (the object is the assembler's own, not GNU's byte for byte).
test_branch_relax_exact.sh	a branch is made long up to three bytes before it need be, where instructions lie between it and its label or another branch does (AS-T-221)
test_branch_relax_label.sh	a branch to an ordinary label of its own section is resolved now, and short where it plainly reaches (AS-T-187); what is left is the two cases at the limit, as in test_branch_relax_exact.sh (AS-T-221)
test_complex_control_32_64.sh	`.uleb128 .LVU23` in .debug_loclists: the view numbers that `.loc ... view` defines are not symbols here (AS-T-400).  It stopped before at a ; inside a comment, which no longer begins a statement
test_diagnostics_determinism.sh	a forced short jump out of range, `jmpb` over 300 bytes, is assembled and should be refused (AS-T-221).  Its messages name the user's file and line now, which is where it stopped before
test_i8086_elf_object.sh	16-bit code is assembled to the wrong bytes (AS-T-347).  test_i8086_corpus.sh, which asks only that the two 16-bit corpora assemble, passes now that a ; in a comment is no statement -- their bytes are not right for it
test_i8086_realmode_core.sh	16-bit code, as above (AS-T-347)
test_isa_heavy_64.sh	vmovdqu is not a mnemonic (AS-T-332)
test_output_binary.sh	code is padded with one-byte NOPs, not GNU's sequences (AS-T-251)
test_sections_symbols_expr_32_64.sh	--64: "failed to emit relocations" for a data relocation narrower than 64 bits (AS-T-210)
test_unit_matrix.sh	runs test_i8086_realmode_core.sh
test_x86_32_corpus_intel_roundtrip.sh	stops at line 251 of the corpus, `fcoms -0x6f70(%bx,%si)`: an x87 instruction with a 16-bit address is refused (AS-T-176).  GNU as 2.46 passes the test: it leaves out the 206 lines of the corpus that GNU refuses, and from the Intel source it makes of objdump's listing what objdump prints and no source says (`repz add`, `lock mov`, `jaw`)
test_x86_64_corpus_intel_roundtrip.sh	the v1, v2 and v3 corpora pass, there and back; stops at line 7115 of the v4 corpus, `{evex} vmovhlps %xmm0,%xmm1,%xmm0`: an AVX instruction asked for in its EVEX form that the AVX-512 tables do not have (AS-T-339).  GNU as 2.46 passes the test, which leaves out of the Intel source what objdump prints and no source says (`repz add`, `jaw`)
test_x86_avx512f_core.sh	EVEX vaddpd is encoded with W = 0 (AS-T-339)
test_x86_avx_core.sh	vpermilps is not encoded (AS-T-327)
